<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Listen! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-style: italic; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's a School Day!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Listen!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Use simple words to express basic needs. interactive: Engage in question-and-answer exchanges using simple words and gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can use simple words to name classroom objects they need (e.g., 'pen' or 'book'). Can express basic needs or ideas using simple phrases with teacher support (e.g., 'Open the book.' 'I need a pencil.'). Can ask and answer basic questions using simple words or gestures (e.g., 'What is it?' with 'Table' or pointing).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, children!" and leads a TPR activity. "Please stand up!" (Teacher models standing up). "Please touch your desk!" (Teacher models touching a desk). "Please listen to the music!" (Teacher turns on a short piece of calm music and points to ears). Teacher encourages students to imitate the actions and repeat the words: 'stand up', 'touch', 'listen'. Teacher emphasizes pronunciation of /s/ in 'stand' and /l/ in 'listen'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of classroom objects (book, chair, desk, table, door, window) and says, "It is a book." "It is a chair." "I need a book." (Teacher points to their mouth and then to the book). Teacher models the initial sound /b/ in 'book' and the syllable stress in 'teacher' (TEA-cher) and 'student' (STU-dent). Teacher also models polite requests: "Please open the book."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using visuals and simple questions.
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a book) "Is this a chair or a book?" (Students point or say 'book').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Mimicking 'listen') "Am I listening or jumping?" (Students mimic 'listening' or say 'listening').</li>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's a School Day!":</b> The teacher presents a large, colorful picture of a Kinder classroom with children, a teacher, chairs, desks, a table, books, a window, and a door. The teacher points to items and asks, "What is it?" and provides the answer, "It is a chair." or "It is a book." Teacher then introduces simple needs: "I need a book." "I need a pencil." using gestures and pointing to actual objects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large classroom poster or projected image, real classroom objects (book, chair, desk, pencil), and flashcards of 'window', 'door', 'teacher', 'student'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Responding questions (e.g., 'What is it?'); Sociolinguistic: Use formal language when giving instructions, such as 'Please stand up.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a chair on the poster: "Is this a table?" (Students shake heads or say 'No').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a book: "What is it?" (Students say 'Book' or point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Touch the window on the picture." (Students point to the window).</li>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Speaking.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Imperatives ('Please open'), Present simple ('It is a...'), WH-questions ('What is it?'). Vocabulary: 'chair', 'desk', 'table', 'book', 'window', 'door', 'teacher', 'student', 'open', 'touch'. Pronunciation: /s/ in 'school', /l/ in 'listen', syllable stress in 'teacher' and 'student'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - "What is it?" Choral Repetition:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards or real objects one by one (chair, desk, book, window, door) and asks, "What is it?" Students respond chorally, "It is a [chair]." The teacher repeats, ensuring clear pronunciation of /s/ in 'desk' and /l/ in 'table', and correct syllable stress for 'teacher' and 'student' when their cards are shown.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - "I need a..." Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher shows an object (e.g., a book) and models "I need a book." Students then complete the sentence when a new object is shown (e.g., a pencil). Teacher points to the object and says, "I need a..." and students complete "pencil." Teacher also practices imperatives: "Please [open] the [book]." Students repeat and perform the action.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - "Touch and Tell":</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair has a set of small classroom object pictures (book, chair, desk, table). Student A gives an instruction to Student B: "Please touch the book." Student B touches the correct picture and says, "Book!" or "I touched the book." Then they switch roles. Teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation as needed, especially for initial sounds like /t/ in 'touch'.</li>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's a School Day!".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Classroom Helpers Role-play." Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group has a selection of real classroom objects or large picture cards (book, pencil, chair, door, window, desk). One student acts as the "teacher" or "helper" and gives instructions to the others. Other students respond by performing the action and/or verbally acknowledging. Students take turns being the "teacher" or "helper."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains the "Classroom Helpers" game. "Today, we are all helpers! We will tell our friends what to do."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In groups, Student A picks an object and gives an instruction using "Please," e.g., "Please open the book!" or "Please touch the table!" (Pragmatic: Describing actions, Sociolinguistic: Politeness and formality).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B performs the action and can respond with a simple word or phrase, e.g., "Book!" or "Okay."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A can also ask, "What is it?" about an object, and Student B replies, "It is a [chair]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students rotate roles. The teacher circulates, observing fluency and encouraging all students to speak and interact.</li>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during the "Classroom Helpers Role-play."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "Classroom Helpers Role-play" by giving and responding to simple instructions and questions about classroom objects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., 'book', 'listen', 'teacher', 'student').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (at least 3-4 words) to name objects or express needs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to give simple commands using 'Please' (e.g., "Please open the book.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to answer "What is it?" questions with simple words (e.g., "Table.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engagement and collaboration with peers in the activity.</li>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher asks students to show thumbs up/down or orally respond.
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you say 'book'?" (Thumbs up if yes).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you ask your friend to 'Please stand up'?" (Thumbs up if yes).</li>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you say today?" (Teacher elicits responses like 'book', 'chair').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or a little difficult to ask your friend to 'Please open the door'?" (Students can show happy/sad face or say 'easy'/'difficult').</li>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The "Classroom Helpers Role-play" worked well for encouraging peer interaction and using imperatives with 'Please'. Most students were able to produce 1-3 word utterances and follow instructions. Some struggled with asking complete WH-questions independently, requiring more modeling and prompting. Pronunciation of 'teacher' and 'student' will need continued practice. The TPR activities were very effective for engagement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> For the next lesson (Writing), students will build on this speaking practice by tracing and copying words for classroom objects and writing simple phrases like "It is a book" or "I need a pencil" with visual support, reinforcing the vocabulary and sentence structures they practiced orally today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>